   "l": "All under heaven has a beginning,\ntake it, then, for the mother of all under heaven.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "There is a beginning to all under heaven, and that beginning is its mother.",
   "n": "以 again with nothing after it. The dropped word is 之, the beginning just named, and it belongs between 以 and 為: 以[之]為天下母, 'take it for the mother of all under heaven'. Wang Bi reads it so — 則可以為天下母矣, 'then it can serve as the mother of all under heaven'. You met the same economy in chapters 1 and 16.\n\nBeginning, mother and the Way are one thing said three ways in this book. Chapter 1 called the named 'the mother of the ten thousand things'; chapter 25 said of the Way that it could be taken for 'the mother of all under heaven', in almost these words.",
   "p": true
  },
