   "l": "Having got its mother,\nthereby you know its children;\nhaving known its children,\ngo back again and guard its mother —\nto the sinking of the body, no peril.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Once you have the mother you know her children; and knowing the children, go back and keep to the mother — and to the end of your days you come to no harm.",
   "n": "沒身不殆 closes chapter 16 in exactly these four characters. It is an idiom, not a warning about drowning: 'the body sinks' for 'as long as one lives'.\n\n復 is the adverb 'again' here, standing before its verb — the same character that was the full verb 'to return' in chapter 16's 復命. It is the adverb again in 復歸 later in this chapter, where 歸 carries the returning, exactly as it did in chapter 16's 各復歸其根.\n\nWhose mother and whose children the text does not say, and it does not need to: the mother is the beginning named in the unit above, the children are the ten thousand things that come of it."
  },
