   "l": "Block up its openings,\nshut its gate;",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Stop up the openings, close the gate;",
   "n": "兌 is a hard word. In the Yijing it is the trigram of the marsh and of the mouth, and here the commentators take it for the body's holes — eyes, ears, mouth — through which wanting gets out and the world gets in. Wang Bi keeps it abstract: 兌 is 'where affairs and desires arise from', 門 'where they come in by'.\n\n其 has no stated antecedent, as so often. Grammatically it is 'its'; in practice what is being shut is one's own senses. These two lines come back word for word in chapter 56."
  },
